<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN"
  "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  com.macro.closepass — the evening close pass's PRIMARY clock (Breathing PR-B).

  Template. Installed by scripts/install_closepass_launchd.sh, which substitutes
  __HOME__ and __SUPPORT_DIR__ and copies it to ~/Library/LaunchAgents/.
  DO NOT load it directly out of the repo — the placeholders are not paths.

  WHY THIS EXISTS. .github/workflows/close-pass.yml used to be the product clock
  and cannot be one. Measured Friday 2026-08-14: the 20:25 UTC cron line's run
  was CREATED at 20:52 — 27 minutes of scheduler drift before a runner was even
  asked for — and its DST sibling at 21:47 then sat 95 minutes in the queue on
  the two-host `macstudio` pool. The board published ~19:20 ET against a
  16:15 ET product target. Estate-wide the same measurement is worse: GitHub
  cron gaps of 90 min to 3 h 12 m (agentos/decisions/DEC-LER-LIVE-LANE-VPS-5MIN-REST.md),
  which is why Live Entry Radar's live lane runs off a VPS timer too. The
  workflow stays armed as a BOUNDED BACKSTOP; this is the clock.

  TIMING — 13:00 local, Monday to Friday, and that IS 16:00 ET year-round.
    The Mac is PT (ops/launchd/com.macro.chainheat.plist says the same thing in
    its own words: "Mac is PT (ET - 3h year-round)"). PT and ET are both US
    zones and flip on the SAME instant in both directions, so the offset between
    them is a constant -3 h through PDT/EDT and PST/EST alike:
      PDT (Mar-Nov)  13:00 PT = 20:00 UTC = 16:00 EDT
      PST (Nov-Mar)  13:00 PT = 21:00 UTC = 16:00 EST
    That is the whole reason a LOCAL StartCalendarInterval is correct here and a
    UTC cron needs a DST pair: launchd calendar entries are local, so one entry
    covers both regimes and there is no off-season line to self-skip.

    The runner then holds for the closes itself (16:00:10 -> 16:12 ET) and the
    session-day question is answered by lib.nyse_calendar inside the checkout —
    so "when is the market open" still has exactly one definition in this lane,
    and this file carries no ET literal, no holiday list, and no deadline.

  WHAT RUNS. __SUPPORT_DIR__/close_pass_host_runner.py, executed by the system
  /usr/bin/python3 (the runner's outer half is stdlib-only, deliberately). The
  copy under Application Support is HOST-INSTALLED PLUMBING: launchd cannot exec
  out of ~/Documents at all, and the primary checkout is routinely parked on an
  old commit. The POLICY it runs is never this vintage — the runner resets a
  locked worktree to origin/main on every run and publishes from there.

  RunAtLoad is false ON PURPOSE: bootstrapping this agent at 11:00 must not fire
  a close pass at 11:00.

  INSTALL / UNINSTALL / TEST — see scripts/install_closepass_launchd.sh. In short:
    bash scripts/install_closepass_launchd.sh
    launchctl kickstart gui/$(id -u)/com.macro.closepass      # manual run
    launchctl bootout   gui/$(id -u)/com.macro.closepass      # stop
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key>
  <string>com.macro.closepass</string>

  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>/usr/bin/python3</string>
    <string>__SUPPORT_DIR__/close_pass_host_runner.py</string>
  </array>

  <!-- 13:00 local (PT) = 16:00 ET in BOTH DST regimes; weekdays only, because a
       NYSE session never falls on a weekend. Holidays are the runner's job, not
       the schedule's: it asks lib.nyse_calendar and exits before any network. -->
  <key>StartCalendarInterval</key>
  <array>
    <dict>
      <key>Weekday</key><integer>1</integer>
      <key>Hour</key><integer>13</integer>
      <key>Minute</key><integer>0</integer>
    </dict>
    <dict>
      <key>Weekday</key><integer>2</integer>
      <key>Hour</key><integer>13</integer>
      <key>Minute</key><integer>0</integer>
    </dict>
    <dict>
      <key>Weekday</key><integer>3</integer>
      <key>Hour</key><integer>13</integer>
      <key>Minute</key><integer>0</integer>
    </dict>
    <dict>
      <key>Weekday</key><integer>4</integer>
      <key>Hour</key><integer>13</integer>
      <key>Minute</key><integer>0</integer>
    </dict>
    <dict>
      <key>Weekday</key><integer>5</integer>
      <key>Hour</key><integer>13</integer>
      <key>Minute</key><integer>0</integer>
    </dict>
  </array>

  <!-- git and the Homebrew python3.12 the lane venv is built from. Secrets are
       NEVER here: the plist is world-readable. The runner sources the primary
       checkout's chmod-600 .env into the subprocess environment instead. -->
  <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
  <dict>
    <key>PATH</key>
    <string>/opt/homebrew/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin</string>
  </dict>

  <key>RunAtLoad</key>
  <false/>

  <!-- Below the render pools on purpose: this lane is ~20 minutes of price-store
       reads and must never win a CPU fight with a nightly bake. -->
  <key>Nice</key>
  <integer>5</integer>

  <key>StandardOutPath</key>
  <string>__HOME__/Library/Logs/macro_closepass/launchd.out.log</string>
  <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
  <string>__HOME__/Library/Logs/macro_closepass/launchd.err.log</string>
</dict>
</plist>
